👍 Yes. I’ll make 3C V1.1 as the clean engineering version, incorporating the checker corrections—not just append them as extra sections. I’ll keep the useful mechanisms and remove repetition/over-engineering.

CNS — LOG 3C

WORK, ORCHESTRATION & AGENT FABRIC

Engineering Construction Baseline V1.1

Status: ENGINEERING BASELINE — CHECKED & CORRECTED
Depends on: Log 1 Constitution + Log 2 Capability/Mechanism Construction + 3A Foundation & Runtime Fabric + 3B Brain & Cognitive Architecture
Role: CNS work-control layer


---

3C.1 — Purpose

3C constructs the machinery that converts CNS intent into coordinated, controlled work.

INTENT
 ↓
ADMIT
 ↓
UNDERSTAND WORK
 ↓
DECOMPOSE IF JUSTIFIED
 ↓
MAP CAPABILITIES
 ↓
SELECT METHODS
 ↓
SELECT RESOURCES
 ↓
CHECK CAPACITY / AUTHORITY / RISK
 ↓
BUILD DEPENDENCIES
 ↓
SCHEDULE
 ↓
EXECUTE
 ↓
SUPERVISE
 ↓
VALIDATE RESULTS
 ↓
GLOBAL COMPLETION CHECK
 ↓
COMPLETE / REPLAN / RECOVER / ESCALATE
 ↓
OUTCOME
 ↓
LEARNING

3C does not itself provide intelligence, memory, models, bots or engines. It controls how available capabilities are used.


---

3C.2 — Fundamental Principle

> CNS must coordinate work, not manufacture agents.



A work item may be executed by:

deterministic function
engine
bot
ML system
LLM
agent
temporary sub-agent
tool
parallel resources
human
combination of resources

Therefore:

TASK ≠ AGENT
MORE AGENTS ≠ MORE INTELLIGENCE

The simplest resource capable of completing the task is preferred unless additional resources produce measurable value.


---

3C.3 — Work Object

3A defines the base WorkItem.

3C extends its operational control:

WorkControl {
    work_id
    intent_id
    parent_id

    required_capabilities[]
    candidate_methods[]
    selected_method

    candidate_resources[]
    selected_resource

    dependencies[]
    dependents[]

    priority
    consequence_level

    cost_budget
    latency_budget
    resource_budget

    completion_criteria[]
    stop_conditions[]

    retry_policy
    supervision_policy

    status
    attempt
    lease_id

    progress_state
    result_refs[]
    evidence_refs[]

    failure_state
    provenance
}


---

3C.4 — Work Admission Control

Not every request immediately becomes executable work.

INTENT
 ↓
VALIDATE
 ↓
AUTHORITY CHECK
 ↓
DUPLICATE WORK CHECK
 ↓
CAPABILITY CHECK
 ↓
CAPACITY CHECK
 ↓
RISK / BUDGET CHECK
 ↓
ADMIT / DELAY / REJECT / ESCALATE

Admission prevents CNS from accepting work that it cannot safely or economically execute.

Possible outcomes:

ADMITTED
QUEUED
DELAYED
REJECTED
ESCALATED


---

3C.5 — Work Decomposition

Decomposition is optional.

CNS first asks:

Can one existing resource complete this correctly?

If yes:

USE ONE RESOURCE

If not:

DECOMPOSE

Decomposition considers:

reliability gain
specialization gain
parallelism gain
verification gain
context isolation
resource efficiency

versus

coordination cost
latency
financial cost
handoff risk
duplication
failure complexity

Conceptually:

\[
DecompositionValue =
Benefit - CoordinationCost
\]

Decomposition occurs only when expected value is positive.


---

3C.6 — Work Graph

For substantial work, CNS may construct a dynamic dependency graph.

WorkGraph {
    graph_id
    intent_id
    root_work_id
    nodes[]
    dependencies[]
    completion_policy
    supervision_policy
    escalation_policy
    budgets
    version
}

Simple work does not require an artificial graph.


---

3C.7 — Dynamic Replanning

The work graph is not permanent.

New evidence may produce:

new task
removed task
changed dependency
new resource
different method
different priority
new verification requirement

Flow:

OBSERVE
 ↓
DEVIATION / NEW INFORMATION
 ↓
DIAGNOSE
 ↓
REPLAN
 ↓
UPDATE WORK GRAPH
 ↓
RESUME

The original plan is subordinate to the actual objective.


---

3C.8 — Task Types

Initial task types:

OBSERVE
RETRIEVE
CALCULATE
CLASSIFY
EXTRACT
RESEARCH
ANALYZE
PREDICT
COMPARE
VERIFY
CHALLENGE
SIMULATE
BACKTEST
EXECUTE
MONITOR
SYNTHESIZE
RECONCILE
RECOVER

New types can be added when a real capability requires them.


---

3C.9 — Capability Matching

For every admitted task:

TASK
 ↓
REQUIRED CAPABILITIES
 ↓
CAPABILITY REGISTRY
 ↓
CANDIDATE METHODS
 ↓
CANDIDATE RESOURCES

Capability, method and resource remain separate:

Capability = what must be done
Method     = how it can be done
Resource   = what actually performs it

This separation is critical for future replacement.


---

3C.10 — Method Selection

CNS should not automatically select a resource before considering the method.

Example:

Task: detect abnormal financial behaviour

Methods:
    deterministic ratio analysis
    statistical anomaly detection
    ML model
    specialist research
    multi-resource investigation

CNS selects the appropriate method according to:

task characteristics
data availability
consequence
uncertainty
historical method performance
cost
latency
resource health

Method performance becomes part of future orchestration learning.


---

3C.11 — Resource Selection

Candidate resources are filtered by hard constraints first:

capability
authority
privacy
risk
compatibility
availability
health

Then ranked by:

quality
reliability
historical performance
calibration
latency
cost
freshness
domain/regime performance

Conceptually:

\[
r^*=\arg\max_r U(r|x)
\]

subject to all hard constraints.

No resource receives authority merely because it has higher intelligence or model capability.


---

3C.12 — Capacity Management

A resource may be suitable but currently unavailable because it is overloaded.

ResourceCapacity {
    resource_id
    concurrency_limit
    compute_budget
    memory_limit
    queue_limit
    reserved_capacity
    current_load
}

Selection therefore becomes:

CAPABILITY FIT
 ↓
HEALTH
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
CAPACITY
 ↓
QUALITY / COST / LATENCY

If capacity is insufficient:

WAIT
ALTERNATIVE RESOURCE
REDUCE PARALLELISM
REPLAN
ESCALATE


---

3C.13 — Backpressure

When incoming work exceeds available capacity:

WORKLOAD ↑
 ↓
CAPACITY PRESSURE
 ↓
QUEUE / DELAY / REDUCE PARALLELISM
 ↓
RESOURCE RECOVERY
 ↓
RESUME

Backpressure prevents CNS from destabilizing itself by accepting unlimited work.


---

3C.14 — Dependency Control

Every dependent task must declare its dependencies.

Example:

Financial Analysis
       ↓
Valuation
       ↓
Portfolio Impact

Independent tasks may run concurrently:

Financials ───────┐
Management ───────┤
Industry ─────────┤
Competitors ──────┼→ Global Synthesis
Technical ────────┤
News ─────────────┘


---

3C.15 — Dependency Safety

CNS must detect:

dependency cycle
missing producer
impossible dependency
permanently blocked task
invalid dependency state

Example:

A → B
B → C
C → A

must not be allowed to remain active indefinitely.

The system must:

detect
diagnose
restructure
or escalate


---

3C.16 — Scheduling

Scheduler considers:

dependency readiness
priority
consequence
deadline
critical-path importance
information value
resource availability
cost
latency
risk

Scheduling must not allow lower-priority work to become permanently starved.

Conceptually:

\[
EffectivePriority =
BasePriority + Aging + DeadlinePressure + Criticality
\]

The exact policy remains configurable.


---

3C.17 — Parallel Execution

Parallelism is allowed when:

tasks are sufficiently independent
resources are available
shared-state conflicts are controlled
coordination cost is justified

Parallelism is not automatically beneficial.

CNS may deliberately execute sequentially when dependencies, risk or shared state require it.


---

3C.18 — Atomic Work Claim

Two workers must never legitimately acquire the same task simultaneously.

Required transition:

READY
 ↓ atomic claim
ASSIGNED
 ↓
LEASED
 ↓
RUNNING

The claim must be atomic at the persistence/control boundary.

This prevents duplicate ownership.


---

3C.19 — Work Lease

3A defines the lease structure.

3C uses it to control worker ownership:

Lease {
    lease_id
    work_id
    task_id
    resource_id
    issued_at
    expires_at
    attempt
}

If a worker disappears:

LEASE EXPIRES
 ↓
INSPECT PARTIAL WORK
 ↓
RESUME / RETRY / REASSIGN / REPLAN

No work silently disappears.


---

3C.20 — Retry Policy

Retry must be controlled.

RetryPolicy {
    max_attempts
    backoff
    retryable_failures[]
    non_retryable_failures[]
    alternate_resource_after[]
    escalation_after[]
}

Examples:

network timeout → retry
temporary provider failure → alternate resource
invalid schema → rework/reject
security failure → stop/escalate
unknown external execution state → reconcile first

Never:

FAIL → RETRY → FAIL → RETRY → ∞


---

3C.21 — Agent Contract

Agents are bounded execution resources.

Agent {
    agent_id
    version
    purpose
    capabilities[]
    accepted_task_types[]

    input_schema
    output_schema

    authority_scope
    resource_limits
    time_limits

    tools[]
    state_scope

    health
    performance
    failure_modes[]
}

Agents do not own global CNS authority.


---

3C.22 — Agent Lifecycle

DISCOVER
 ↓
CONFIGURE
 ↓
VALIDATE
 ↓
READY
 ↓
ASSIGN
 ↓
RUN
 ↓
REPORT
 ↓
RESULT VALIDATION
 ↓
RELEASE / RETAIN

Failure:

RUN
 ↓
FAIL
 ↓
RECOVER / RETRY / REASSIGN
 ↓
QUARANTINE if necessary

Temporary agents may be destroyed after their useful work is complete.


---

3C.23 — Selective Sub-Agent Creation

Sub-agent creation is itself a work-control decision.

NEED
 ↓
EXISTING RESOURCE SUFFICIENT?
 ├── YES → USE IT
 └── NO
      ↓
NEW SPECIALIST JUSTIFIED?
 ├── NO → ALTERNATIVE METHOD
 └── YES → CREATE BOUNDED WORKER

Creation requires:

purpose
capability
scope
authority
budget
deadline
inputs
completion criteria
termination condition


---

3C.24 — Spawn Protection

Every temporary worker carries:

parent_work_id
parent_agent_id
depth
spawn_budget

CNS enforces:

maximum depth
maximum workers
maximum runtime
maximum cost
maximum branching

Prevents:

agent → agent → agent → agent → ...

from becoming uncontrolled.


---

3C.25 — Supervision

Supervision begins with cheap deterministic checks:

assignment correct?
started?
heartbeat alive?
dependency satisfied?
output schema valid?
evidence present?
deadline respected?
resource healthy?
completion criteria satisfied?

Only when interpretation is necessary:

DETERMINISTIC CHECK
 ↓
AMBIGUITY
 ↓
SPECIALIST / LLM JUDGMENT

A permanent supervisor agent is not required.


---

3C.26 — Progress

Work reports:

completed_tasks
remaining_tasks
blocked_tasks
failed_tasks
evidence_collected
expected_output
actual_output
quality
uncertainty
resource_health
estimated_remaining_cost
estimated_remaining_time

But:

> Progress percentage is never equivalent to completion.



One missing critical fact can make “99% complete” unusable.


---

3C.27 — Result Acceptance Gate

A worker's completion does not automatically mean its result is accepted.

RESULT
 ↓
SCHEMA VALIDATION
 ↓
COMPLETENESS
 ↓
PROVENANCE
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
QUALITY
 ↓
ACCEPT / REWORK / REJECT

Accepted results become eligible for downstream work.

Rejected results remain traceable and may trigger rework.


---

3C.28 — Structured Result Contract

WorkResult {
    result_id
    work_id
    resource_id
    method_id

    output
    evidence_refs[]
    assumptions[]
    uncertainty
    confidence
    limitations[]

    provenance
    timestamp
    status
}

This prevents outputs such as:

> “Looks bullish.”



from becoming machine-trusted conclusions without context.


---

3C.29 — Work Handoff

A handoff must communicate:

WHAT WAS FOUND
WHAT WAS NOT FOUND
ASSUMPTIONS
UNCERTAINTIES
EVIDENCE
LIMITATIONS
WHAT SHOULD HAPPEN NEXT

Therefore:

Worker A
 ↓
Structured Result
 ↓
Worker B

rather than uncontrolled context passing.


---

3C.30 — Global Completion

Critical distinction:

LOCAL COMPLETION ≠ GLOBAL COMPLETION

A worker can say:

> “My task is finished.”



CNS must separately determine:

> “Is the original objective sufficiently completed?”



Global completion checks:

goal coverage
required evidence
critical dependencies
contradictions
risk coverage
missing capabilities
output quality
decision readiness
stop conditions

Possible result:

COMPLETE
INVESTIGATE_MORE
REPLAN
ESCALATE
STOP


---

3C.31 — Cross-Worker Contradiction

Suppose:

Bot A → bullish
ML-3 → bullish
Agent B → bullish

Financial analysis → weak
Cash flow → deteriorating
Management research → contradiction

CNS does not vote:

3 bullish vs 3 negative

Instead:

CONTRADICTION
 ↓
SOURCE CHECK
 ↓
TIME CHECK
 ↓
DEFINITION CHECK
 ↓
EVIDENCE CHECK
 ↓
INDEPENDENCE CHECK
 ↓
INVESTIGATE

Contradiction becomes an investigation signal.


---

3C.32 — Adaptive Replanning

Replanning may be triggered by:

new evidence
failed resource
invalid assumption
contradiction
changed deadline
budget pressure
capability gap
unexpected result
task becoming irrelevant

DEVIATION
 ↓
DIAGNOSE
 ↓
MODIFY WORK GRAPH
 ↓
REASSIGN / ADD / REMOVE / REORDER WORK
 ↓
RESUME


---

3C.33 — Cancellation Propagation

When work is cancelled, CNS determines what happens to dependent/child work.

ROOT CANCEL
 ↓
DEPENDENCY ANALYSIS
 ↓
┌─────────────┬──────────────┐
CANCEL       DETACH         FINISH

A child task may continue if its result remains independently valuable.

Otherwise it is cancelled.

No orphan work should remain without an owner.


---

3C.34 — Computational Reuse

Before creating new work:

DO WE ALREADY HAVE A VALID RESULT?

Check:

input identity
data version
method version
resource version
timestamp
freshness
validity conditions
provenance

If valid:

REUSE

Otherwise:

RECOMPUTE

This implements Log 2 CAP-020.


---

3C.35 — Work Supervision vs Brain

3B provides cognitive judgment.

3C provides work control.

3B:
What should we investigate?

3C:
How will we investigate it?

3D:
Which resources can do it?

3E:
What should be remembered?

3F:
Can we trust the result?

This prevents architectural overlap.


---

3C.36 — Whole-Ecosystem Work Loop

The complete mechanism is:

CNS
                          │
                      UNDERSTAND
                          │
                    ADMISSION CONTROL
                          │
                 DECOMPOSE IF JUSTIFIED
                          │
                  CAPABILITY MATCH
                          │
                   METHOD SELECT
                          │
                  RESOURCE SELECT
                          │
             CAPACITY / AUTHORITY / RISK
                          │
                 DEPENDENCY CONTROL
                          │
                     SCHEDULE
                          │
                 ATOMIC CLAIM + LEASE
                          │
                      EXECUTE
                          │
                     SUPERVISE
                          │
                 RESULT VALIDATION
                          │
                      COLLECT
                          │
                GLOBAL COMPLETION?
                    /            \
                  NO              YES
                  │                │
               REPLAN          SYNTHESIZE
                  │                │
             MORE WORK             │
                  └───────┬────────┘
                          ↓
                       OUTCOME
                          ↓
                       LEARN

This is the actual work machine of CNS.


---

3C.37 — Failure Handling

FAILURE
 ↓
CLASSIFY
 ↓
RESUME?
 ├─ YES → RESUME
 └─ NO
      ↓
ALTERNATIVE RESOURCE?
 ├─ YES → REASSIGN
 └─ NO
      ↓
ALTERNATIVE METHOD?
 ├─ YES → REPLAN
 └─ NO
      ↓
CAPABILITY GAP?
 ├─ YES → GAP RESEARCH
 └─ NO
      ↓
ESCALATE / STOP

Unknown failures are not endlessly retried.


---

3C.38 — Work Priority & Starvation Protection

Scheduler must balance:

priority
deadline
consequence
critical path
dependency importance
age
resource availability

Aging prevents low-priority work from being permanently starved.

Critical financial work can receive stronger scheduling priority according to policy and authority.


---

3C.39 — Work Learning

CNS records orchestration outcomes:

task
method
resource
conditions
execution
result
quality
cost
latency
failure
outcome

Then learns:

Which method works?
Which resource works?
Under what conditions?
At what cost?
With what reliability?
Which decomposition patterns help?
Which create waste?
Which resource combinations create useful results?

This feeds future resource and method selection.


---

3C.40 — Orchestration Policy

OrchestrationPolicy {
    version

    admission_rules
    decomposition_rules
    routing_rules
    capacity_rules
    parallelism_rules

    scheduling_rules
    spawn_rules

    supervision_rules
    retry_rules
    escalation_rules

    completion_rules
    cancellation_rules
    budget_rules
    failure_rules
}

Policies are versioned and controlled.


---

3C.41 — Runtime Implementation Boundary

Conceptual CNS work fabric:

CNS WORK FABRIC
│
├── Work Admission
├── Work Graph
├── Task Manager
├── Capability Matcher
├── Method Selector
├── Resource Selector
├── Capacity Manager
├── Dependency Manager
├── Scheduler
├── Lease Manager
├── Worker / Agent Manager
├── Supervisor
├── Result Validator
├── Replanner
├── Completion Checker
└── Work Metrics

These are logical boundaries, not mandatory separate services.

Initial implementation may remain compact.


---

3C.42 — Core Interfaces

Conceptual:

trait WorkController {
    create_work(...)
    admit(...)
    decompose(...)
    match_capabilities(...)
    select_methods(...)
    select_resources(...)
    check_capacity(...)
    schedule(...)
    claim(...)
    assign(...)
    supervise(...)
    validate_result(...)
    collect(...)
    replan(...)
    cancel(...)
    complete(...)
}

Agent:

trait Agent {
    capabilities(...)
    accept(...)
    execute(...)
    report(...)
    health(...)
    shutdown(...)
}

Resource selection:

trait ResourceSelector {
    candidates(...)
    score(...)
    select(...)
}


---

3C.43 — Security / Authority Boundary

3C never grants authority merely because a worker is selected.

Workers cannot automatically:

change constitution
change risk limits
deploy themselves
modify core policy
move capital
bypass verification
approve their own modification
access unrelated secrets

All consequential actions remain subject to 3A authority, validation and financial safety boundaries.


---

3C.44 — Acceptance Tests

WORK-001  Create work from intent
WORK-002  Admit/reject work correctly
WORK-003  Decompose only when justified
WORK-004  Match required capabilities
WORK-005  Select valid methods
WORK-006  Select valid resources
WORK-007  Respect capacity
WORK-008  Apply backpressure
WORK-009  Respect dependencies
WORK-010  Detect dependency cycles
WORK-011  Execute safe parallel work
WORK-012  Prevent duplicate task ownership
WORK-013  Recover worker failure
WORK-014  Apply retry policy correctly
WORK-015  Prevent uncontrolled spawning
WORK-016  Validate worker results
WORK-017  Detect contradictions
WORK-018  Detect missing evidence
WORK-019  Replan after new evidence
WORK-020  Distinguish local/global completion
WORK-021  Prevent starvation
WORK-022  Propagate cancellation correctly
WORK-023  Reuse valid computation
WORK-024  Recover interrupted work
WORK-025  Preserve provenance
WORK-026  Respect authority
WORK-027  Never bypass financial safety controls


---

3C.45 — Performance Measurements

Measure:

goal completion
task success
unnecessary work
duplicate work
decomposition quality
resource-selection quality
handoff failures
contradiction detection
missing-evidence detection
replanning success
worker recovery
global completion accuracy
latency
cost
resource utilization
queue pressure
human intervention

Most important:

> Does orchestration produce better CNS outcomes than a simpler baseline?




---

3C.46 — Baseline Requirement

Every sophisticated mechanism must demonstrate measurable benefit over an appropriate simpler baseline.

Examples:

single worker
fixed workflow
static resource selection
no parallelism
no replanning
no temporary sub-agents

If a complex mechanism does not improve the actual objective:

REMOVE / SIMPLIFY


---

3C.47 — Explicit Non-Assumptions

3C does not require:

agent swarm

hundreds of permanent agents

manager LLM

recursive spawning

decomposition of every task

parallelism everywhere

LLM supervision everywhere

majority voting as truth

unrestricted agent communication

graph database

distributed message broker at initial scale

complex workflow platform

one permanent orchestration algorithm.


Complexity must earn its place through measured value.


---

3C.48 — Relationship With CNS

3A → FOUNDATION
      ↓
3B → BRAIN
      ↓
3C → WORK CONTROL
      ↓
3D → RESOURCES
      ↓
3E → MEMORY / EXPERIENCE
      ↓
3F → VERIFICATION / RESEARCH / DECISION
      ↓
3G → SELF-IMPROVEMENT / RECOVERY

Actual CNS operation is cyclic:

BRAIN
 ↕
WORK
 ↕
RESOURCES
 ↕
MEMORY
 ↕
VERIFICATION
 ↕
OUTCOME
 ↕
LEARNING
 ↺


---

3C.49 — Checker Approval

The independent checker specifically tested for:

Agent explosion → controlled.
Manager-LLM dependency → rejected.
Unnecessary decomposition → prevented.
Infinite retries → prevented.
Resource overload → capacity + backpressure.
Deadlocks → dependency-cycle detection.
Duplicate workers → atomic claim + lease.
Starvation → aging/priority mechanism.
Bad worker output → result acceptance gate.
Orphaned work → cancellation propagation.
Worker completion mistaken for global completion → explicitly prevented.
Contradictions averaged away → explicitly prevented.
New evidence ignored → replanning.
Resource failure → recovery/reassignment.
Authority escape → 3A boundary.
Unnecessary infrastructure → explicitly rejected.

Verdict:

> ACCEPTED AS CNS LOG 3C ENGINEERING CONSTRUCTION BASELINE V1.1.



No further structural change is justified at this point.


---

3C.50 — Final Construction Principle

> CNS does not become intelligent merely because it has many workers. CNS becomes intelligent when it can understand what work is required, determine why that work matters, choose the right capability and method, use the right resources, control capacity and dependencies, verify the results, recognize failure and contradiction, replan when reality changes, and determine when the whole objective—not merely an individual task—is actually complete.



